\description{
Constrained ordination of samples from their feature abundances: the
axes of a (normalized) count table that sample metadata explain, followed
by principal components of what they leave unexplained. Gives the same
results as \code{vegan::rda()}, computed straight from sparse input without
forming any \eqn{n \times n}{n x n} or dense table, so it takes far less
time and memory for large datasets. With \code{norm = 'hellinger'} or
\code{norm = 'chord'} it is transformation-based RDA (tb-RDA); with
\code{Condition()} terms, partial RDA.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}

With \eqn{Z} the normalized table (samples in rows), \eqn{J} the
centering matrix, and \eqn{D} the diagonal matrix of the features'
standard deviations (or the identity when \code{scale = FALSE}), RDA (Rao
1964; Legendre & Legendre 2012, §11.1) analyzes the centered table
\eqn{Y = JZD^{-1} / \sqrt{n - 1}}. With \eqn{H} the projection onto the
(centered) constraints, after removing any conditions (Borcard et al.
1992), the constrained axes are the principal components of the fitted
values \eqn{HY}, and the unconstrained axes those of the residuals
\eqn{(I - H)Y}. Each axis's eigenvalue is a variance, so they add up to
the total variance of \eqn{Y}.

\code{rda()} shares its solver with \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}: since \eqn{YY^T} is Gower's
centered matrix of the Euclidean distances between the rows of \eqn{Y},
the constrained axes come from a small eigenproblem with one row per
constraint column, and the unconstrained axes, when needed, from block
Lanczos iteration. Both need only products of the table with a few
vectors at a time, computed in parallel from the non-zero values alone,
as in \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, so a sparse table is never made dense.

\strong{Transformation-based RDA}

RDA of raw counts measures differences between samples by Euclidean
distances, which suit species abundance data poorly (Legendre & Gallagher
2001). The Hellinger and chord transformations fix that:
\code{rda(counts, ..., norm = 'hellinger')} is an RDA that preserves Hellinger
distances between samples, and \code{norm = 'chord'} chord distances. \code{norm = 'clr'} (with a \code{pseudocount}) preserves Aitchison distances.

\strong{Relation to dbrda(), pca() and permanova()}
\itemize{
\item \code{rda(counts, ..., norm = 'hellinger')} has the same axes, \code{prop},
\eqn{R^2} and biplot scores as
\code{dbrda(euclidean(counts, norm = 'hellinger'), ...)}: db-RDA of
Euclidean distances is RDA. \code{dbrda()} analyzes \eqn{YY^T} without
dividing by \eqn{n - 1}, so its \code{trace}, \code{eig}, \code{inertia}, and
\code{constrained_eig} are \code{rda()}'s times \eqn{n - 1}, and its scores
\code{rda()}'s times \eqn{(n - 1)^{1/4}}. When any distance is 4 or more
(see \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}'s "Scale"), as Euclidean distances between raw counts
often are, \code{dbrda()} divides by \eqn{n - 1} too, and all of these are
the same as \code{rda()}'s.
\item With no constraints, \code{rda(counts)} is a PCA: its \code{eig}, \code{prop}, and
\code{trace} are those of \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} with the same \code{norm} and \code{scale}. Its
scores point the same way as \code{pca()}'s, but are in \code{vegan}'s scaling
2: each axis's scores are \code{pca()}'s divided by
\eqn{\sqrt{(n - 1) \cdot eig}} and multiplied by
\eqn{\sqrt[4]{(n - 1) \cdot trace}}.
\item The permutation test of an RDA (\code{vegan::anova.cca()}) is PERMANOVA of
the Euclidean distances between the normalized samples: e.g.
\code{permanova(euclidean(counts, norm = 'hellinger'), 'treatment', meta)}.
Its \eqn{R^2} is \code{rda()}'s \code{r_squared}, and its pseudo-F and
p-value those of \code{anova(vegan::rda(...))} for the same permutations.
}

\code{\link[=varpart]{varpart()}}, \code{\link[=forward_select]{forward_select()}} and \code{\link[=principal_response_curves]{principal_response_curves()}} build
on \code{rda()}.

\strong{Matching vegan}

\code{rda()} gives the same scores, eigenvalues, inertia, \eqn{R^2}, biplot
scores, centroids and species scores as \code{vegan::rda()} on the normalized
table, up to the signs of the axes. Columns of the model matrix that are
linear combinations of earlier ones are dropped, with a message, as in
\code{vegan}. With \code{scale = TRUE}, features with constant values are left out
(their loadings are 0), where \code{vegan} would stop with an error.

\strong{Same names in other packages}

\code{vegan::rda()} shares its name with ecodive's \code{rda()}, and whichever
package was attached last masks the other's. Write \code{ecodive::rda()}, or
use \code{\link[=count_ord]{count_ord()}}, which always runs ecodive's, to be sure.

\strong{Axis signs and reproducibility}

An eigenvector's sign is arbitrary, so different software can return
mirror images of the same axis. \code{rda()} flips each axis so that its
largest score in absolute value is positive, and flips the biplot scores,
centroids and loadings with it. Results are identical from run to run
and for any number of \code{cpus}.
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}, \code{\link[=capda]{capda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them, and last \code{display},
with the ordination's title for \code{plot()} and \code{print()}: the method's
name, and for the methods that take distances, the distance matrix's
title, such as "Principal Coordinates Analysis of Bray-Curtis
Dissimilarity" (see \link{ecodive_display}). The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full, and \code{plot()} draws the
samples on the first two axes, labelled from the attributes, with any
loadings, constraints and centroids on top (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}).
\code{plot(ord, "Species", iris)} colours the samples by a column of
metadata. Subsetting with \code{[}, or \code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain
matrix or data frame without the attributes.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}
or \code{norm = 'log'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation and
in the log of proportions. If \code{norm} is \code{'clr'} or
\code{'log'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}), or
replace the zeros first with \code{\link{replace_zeros}()}, whose
result needs no pseudocount.

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\examples{
    # tb-RDA: RDA of Hellinger-transformed abundances.
    counts <- round(iris[, 1:4] * 10)
    ord    <- rda(counts, 'Species', iris, norm = 'hellinger')
    plot(ord, 'Species', iris)
    attr(ord, 'r_squared')
    attr(ord, 'loadings')

    # Chord-transformed, in vegan's style.
    ord <- rda(counts ~ Species, iris, norm = 'chord', n_axes = 2)
    str(ord)

    # Its permutation test: PERMANOVA of the Hellinger distances.
    permanova(euclidean(counts, norm = 'hellinger'), 'Species', iris, permutations = 99)

    # Without constraints, a PCA.
    rda(counts)

}
